          "heading": "Numerical case studies",
          "content": "## Numerical case studies\n\n### Case 1.1: Elastic tension of a steel tie\n\n**Given:** P=270 kN, L=4.0 m, A=1800 mm\u00b2, E=200 GPa.\n\n**Computation:** \u03c3=P/A=150 MPa; \u03b4=PL/(AE)=3.0 mm.\n\n**Interpretation:** Two independent results are useful: stress isolates material demand, whereas elongation tests an occupant or equipment service limit.\n\n**Independent audit:** Before accepting the numerical answer, identify the dimension of the resulting quantity and confirm it matches the left-hand side of the formula. Replace the nominal input with an independently selected second input and repeat the calculation by hand. A single correct number can conceal an incorrect sign convention, wrong load level, or missing connection effect. State the physical phenomenon left outside the formula.\n\n### Case 1.2: Load conversion before member analysis\n\n**Given:** Area load=2.4 kN/m\u00b2, tributary width=3.2 m, beam span=6.0 m.\n\n**Computation:** w=7.68 kN/m; reactions=23.04 kN each; Mmax=34.56 kN\u00b7m.\n\n**Interpretation:** The area-to-line-load conversion is often a larger source of design error than arithmetic inside the bending formula.\n\n**Independent audit:** Before accepting the numerical answer, identify the dimension of the resulting quantity and confirm it matches the left-hand side of the formula. Replace the nominal input with an independently selected second input and repeat the calculation by hand. A single correct number can conceal an incorrect sign convention, wrong load level, or missing connection effect. State the physical phenomenon left outside the formula."
