          "heading": "Numerical case studies",
          "content": "## Numerical case studies\n\n### Case 2.1: A straight net section through holes\n\n**Given:** b=260 mm, t=12 mm, two holes d=22 mm, Fu=510 MPa.\n\n**Computation:** An=(260\u221244)\u00d712=2592 mm\u00b2; ideal force=1321.92 kN.\n\n**Interpretation:** This ideal force is not design resistance and ignores stagger, shear lag, block shear and connection strength.\n\n**Independent audit:** Before accepting the numerical answer, identify the dimension of the resulting quantity and confirm it matches the left-hand side of the formula. Replace the nominal input with an independently selected second input and repeat the calculation by hand. A single correct number can conceal an incorrect sign convention, wrong load level, or missing connection effect. State the physical phenomenon left outside the formula.\n\n### Case 2.2: Service extension of a long tension rod\n\n**Given:** P=115 kN, L=7500 mm, A=980 mm\u00b2, E=200000 MPa.\n\n**Computation:** \u03b4=PL/(AE)=4.40 mm.\n\n**Interpretation:** Whether this movement is tolerable is a functional question; the yield-force check alone does not answer it.\n\n**Independent audit:** Before accepting the numerical answer, identify the dimension of the resulting quantity and confirm it matches the left-hand side of the formula. Replace the nominal input with an independently selected second input and repeat the calculation by hand. A single correct number can conceal an incorrect sign convention, wrong load level, or missing connection effect. State the physical phenomenon left outside the formula."
