          "id": "sec-5-3",
          "secNumber": "5.3",
          "heading": "Axial force with bending and torsion",
          "content": "## Lesson 5.1: Axial force with bending and torsion\n\n### Physical principle\n\nReal columns and beam-columns rarely carry perfectly concentric compression. Frame sway, eccentric gravity loads, connection geometry and lateral forces create moments together with axial load. The response combines material stress with stability effects, and the interaction cannot generally be verified by adding two unrelated allowable stress values.\n\n### Engineering method and interpretation\n\nSeparate first-order internal actions from second-order amplification. Determine whether the member experiences uniaxial or biaxial flexure, shear and torsion. A section interaction diagram can be useful for understanding competing resistances, but member instability requires separate consideration and code-specific equations.\n\n### Worked engineering illustration\n\nConsider an illustrative reference P_ref=1500 kN and M_ref=410 kN\u00b7m. A demand of P=850 kN, M=180 kN\u00b7m gives a simple unity-sum screen 850/1500+180/410\u22481.006. This index is not an AISC or Eurocode acceptance calculation.\n\n### Model boundary and field check\n\nA load path that appears acceptable when moments are small may become sensitive to bending once the column is slender. The correct interaction check must account for the frame system and limit state.\n\n#### Technical reasoning workbook: axial force with bending and torsion\n\n**Prediction before computation.** Identify the assumed primary action and the geometry or material parameter that governs the response. Write a one-line dimensionally consistent prediction about what happens when that parameter increases while all other inputs stay fixed. Where the response might change sign or mode, identify the physical condition that would trigger the change. Do not label an expected trend as a verified result until the force path and boundary conditions are explicit.\n\n**Independent calculation.** Starting from the illustrated data, reproduce the numerical statement using SI units converted consistently to N and mm. Draw the free-body diagram or a section-force diagram. State an independent check: a reaction sum, a compatibility condition, an equilibrium closure, a limiting case, or a comparison against a fundamental textbook formula. Document numerical precision and avoid reporting more significant figures than the inputs justify.\n\n**Alternative system.** Now consider the same component embedded in a real building, where the adjacent members and connections are not perfectly idealized. List the changes needed in boundary conditions, force transfer, section behavior, construction stage or local detailing. Explain which changes require a revised analysis, which create additional limit states, and which cannot be resolved without the governing design specification. The alternative should remain physically plausible and must not be treated as a certified design.\n\n**Peer-review challenge.** A colleague argues that increasing nominal material strength alone will solve the structural issue addressed by \u201caxial force with bending and torsion.\u201d Examine this assertion by distinguishing stiffness, strength, global instability, local failure and connectivity. Identify at least one regime where the assertion is incomplete. Support the conclusion using the mechanisms developed above, then state what additional evidence would reverse it.\n\n**Communicating results.** Prepare a brief calculation note with assumptions, dimensions, unit conversions, governing action and known exclusions. Every graph should name its x- and y-axis units. If the calculation is used to compare alternatives, state which variables were held constant; otherwise the comparison cannot attribute an observed improvement to one design choice."
